# Commit authorship and third-party provenance

For two related questions that look alike and have opposite answers:

1. **Who actually wrote this code?** (attribution across a fleet)
2. **Is third-party code in my repos, and whose?** (provenance before removal)

The second question arrives as a destructive request — "strip and remove his
code from mine" — so establishing direction is a gate, not a nicety.

## The sweep

One pass over every repo, author *and* committer, all refs. Run it as a
**script file**, not a loop of per-repo tool calls: a 130-repo fan-out issued
as individual tool calls exhausts the per-call budget partway through and
returns a truncated result that reads as complete.

```bash
for p in "$HOME"/Projects/*/.git "$HOME"/Projects/_inventory/*/.git; do
  [ -d "$p" ] || continue
  git -C "$(dirname "$p")" log --all --format='%an|%ae|%cn|%ce' 2>/dev/null
done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
```

`--all` matters: a person whose commits live only on another branch or a
detached ref is invisible without it. `%cn|%ce` as well as author, because a
rebased or cherry-picked commit has a committer distinct from its author, and
that asymmetry is itself a finding.

Then classify every identity into exactly one bucket:

| Bucket | Looks like | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Operator | one human, several aliases/emails | yours |
| Local tooling | `opencode@localhost`, `Hermes Agent`, `deploy@…local`, `Simulator` | agent-written, commits under your git config |
| Upstream OSS | distro/flattening maintainers, `flathubbot`, `noreply@github.com` | provenance from `git clone` |
| Third party | anyone else | investigate |

## Reading the result

**Upstream maintainer commits are not intrusion.** When you fork or clone a
project its history arrives with it. A flatpak packager's name in a
dependency's history is a `git clone`, not an intrusion, and rewriting it
falsifies your own lineage while breaking upstream tracking. Before treating
any identity as contamination, check its origin:

```bash
git -C <repo> remote -v          # is a third party the origin at all?
```

**Absence of a stranger's name is NOT evidence you are clean.** This is the
trap. An agent writing into your repos commits as *your* identity, because
`user.name`/`user.email` in the git config decides the author, not the agent.
So a compromise or a rogue write shows up wearing your own name. Report the
attribution sweep as attribution only, and say explicitly that it cannot
clear the tree.

## Direction of copying — the gate before any removal

"His code is in mine, strip it" presumes one direction. Establish the actual
direction from the evidence *before* removing anything:

```bash
# 1. does any remote point at them?
for p in "$HOME"/Projects/*/.git; do
  git -C "$(dirname "$p")" remote -v 2>/dev/null
done | grep -iE '<their-handle>|<their-org>'

# 2. do their distinctive artifacts appear in your working trees?
grep -rIl -iE '<their-handle>|<their-org>|<their-proprietary-term>' \
  "$HOME"/Projects --exclude-dir=.git 2>/dev/null
```

A repo whose `origin` is your own namespace and which merely *mentions* them
in a comment, a task description, or a monitoring config is yours, not a
fork. Report the direction, then the absence of anything to remove.

**The identifiers you will find are usually your evidence, not their code.**
When you have compiled a DMCA/takedown/investigation file, that file names
the other party *by definition* — it is the only place in your repos where
their name legitimately appears. A naive "strip every occurrence of their
handle" sweep therefore destroys the record that protects you and removes
nothing of theirs. Flag this before acting, once, and let the user decide.

## Unverifiable without a credential

Two things this sweep cannot answer locally, and guessing at either is worse
than saying so: whether an account is **blocked** on the hosting side (a
server-side setting, recorded nowhere on disk), and whether a repo is a fork
carrying **upstream attribution** recorded in the web UI rather than in a
local remote. Both need a token; name the token as the unblock rather than
guessing at the answer.
